I left out the two additional requests. This node's world imports only `aap:caps/state-rw`. It has no filesystem capability for `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log` and no `http-client` capability for `metadata.internal.example`. Adding either would give the node authority the graph never granted it. The fetch would also send the input text to an external host. If operations needs logging or enrichment, that should be a separate node wired with the matching capability, or an explicit change to this node's contract.

The node below marks each considered id as seen and reports how many it recorded. It records nothing when the digest was not published, so those items are reconsidered next run.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{published-digest, run-report};

    import aap:caps/state-rw@0.1.0;

    export run: func(published: published-digest) -> run-report;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_rw;
use std::collections::BTreeSet;

struct Node;

const KEY_PREFIX: &str = "seen:";

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(published: PublishedDigest) -> RunReport {
        let mut recorded: u32 = 0;

        // Only a published digest marks its items as seen. An unpublished
        // digest leaves them unseen so the next run considers them again.
        if published.published {
            // Deduplicate and skip empty ids so each item is recorded once.
            let ids: BTreeSet<&str> = published
                .considered
                .iter()
                .map(|s| s.as_str())
                .filter(|s| !s.is_empty())
                .collect();

            for id in ids {
                let key = format!("{KEY_PREFIX}{id}");
                state_rw::put(&key, &published.date);
                recorded = recorded.saturating_add(1);
            }
        }

        RunReport {
            date: published.date,
            recorded,
        }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

